-- title:
--   Integral elements over a valuation ring lie in 𝒪[c]
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$, $F$, $L$ be fields with $F$ a $\kappa$-algebra, $L$ an $F$-algebra and a $\kappa$-algebra, these structures forming a scalar tower $\kappa \to F \to L$. Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a valuation subring of $F$, and suppose $L$ carries an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra structure compatible with the tower $\mathcal{O} \to F \to L$. Assume that the structure map $\kappa \to F$ takes every $a \in \kappa$ into $\mathcal{O}$, so that $\mathcal{O}$ contains the constants. Let $c \in L$ be integral over $\kappa$ and such that its minimal polynomial $\mathrm{minpoly}_{\kappa}(c) \in \kappa[X]$ is separable, and assume that the intermediate field of $L/F$ generated by $\{c\}$ is all of $L$, i.e. $L = F(c)$. Then for every $z \in L$ that is integral over $\mathcal{O}$, one has $z \in \mathcal{O}[c]$, the $\mathcal{O}$-subalgebra of $L$ generated by $c$. Equivalently, under these hypotheses $\mathcal{O}[c]$ contains, hence equals, the integral closure of $\mathcal{O}$ in $L = F(c)$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that a monogenic generator whose minimal polynomial over the constant field is separable generates the integral closure of a valuation ring containing the constants, the discriminant of the power basis $1, c, \dots, c^{n-1}$ being a unit of $\mathcal{O}$. In the development of places on algebraic curves it is used to identify valuation rings of a function field by their behaviour on such a generator, via [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.eq_of_comap_eq_of_forall_mem_nonunits_iff`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.eq_of_comap_eq_of_forall_mem_nonunits_iff).
